=== MEASURED DECOMPOSITION (cache 6G, warm) ===
  67 slots/layer of 512 experts = 13.1% L1 capacity
  per expert: gate 490K iq2_s + up 490K iq2_s + down 900K iq4_nl = 1.815 MiB
  per token : 480 expert reads = 871 MiB of weights touched
  L1 hit 70% -> ~144 misses/tok = ~260 MiB from RAM or disk
  remap total == stall == 85-135 ms, i.e. the whole token minus ~20 ms
  token time == stall + ~20 ms (GPU compute + graph; GPU util only 17-20%)
  drive, measured unbuffered+overlapped: 0.93 GB/s @512KiB QD1 -> 2.85 GB/s @QD4+
  => at the control config the stall runs at ~90% of the drive's random-read limit
  => 15 tok/s needs stall <= 47 ms; 20 tok/s needs stall <= 30 ms
  The old "1.14 GB/s at expert granularity" figure was a QD1 buffered number; the
  drive is faster than that, but raising the queue depth does not help (see nulls).

=== FIXED BUGS IN THE INHERITED STREAMING CODE (none moved throughput) ===
- Windows pread serialized every read behind one global mutex (seek+read). That is
  also why --moe-stream-io-threads and --moe-stream-direct were both no-ops:
  the mutex pinned queue depth to 1 and `direct` was GGML_UNUSED on Windows.
- host L2 sized every slab to max_nb_expert (900K), wasting 410K on each 490K
  gate/up slab = 30% of the arena.
- host L2 evicted by an O(n_slabs) linear scan under its lock on every insert.
- host L2 held its lock across the slab memcpy, serializing ~237 MiB/tok of copying.

