ROOT CAUSE OF EVERY "NULL" ABOVE
  llama_moe_stream::open_files opened ONE Windows HANDLE per GGUF split
  (FILE_FLAG_RANDOM_ACCESS, no FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED) and every I/O worker issued
  ReadFile through it with an OVERLAPPED offset. A synchronous Windows file object
  holds its lock for the whole of each ReadFile, so those "positional, lock-free,
  concurrent" reads were serialized by the kernel. Queue depth was pinned at 1 per
  file no matter how many workers ran.

  Measured directly (bench/qd.py, 6 threads, random reads over the 46 GiB split):
      448 KiB unbuffered   shared handle 0.86 GB/s   per-thread handles 2.21 GB/s
      896 KiB unbuffered   shared handle 1.31 GB/s   per-thread handles 2.71 GB/s
      448 KiB buffered     shared handle 1.81 GB/s   per-thread handles 5.80 GB/s
      896 KiB buffered     shared handle 3.03 GB/s   per-thread handles 11.49 GB/s

  This is why --moe-stream-io-threads, splitting an expert into three parallel
  weight loads, FILE_FLAG_RANDOM_ACCESS and removing the old global io_mtx all
  measured as no-ops: none of them could raise a queue depth the file object held
