=== THE PAGE-LOCKED HOT-EXPERT FILE (the second real win) ===
Why it exists: after the handle fix the remaining stall is not SSD bandwidth. Delivering one
byte of expert weight to VRAM out of the OS page cache costs THREE passes over DRAM -
ReadFile memcpies it into pinned staging, and the DMA then reads those bytes back. On
DDR4-2400 that is the binding resource, not the drive.

The blocker was believed to be fatal: ggml's own host registration asks for
cudaHostRegisterReadOnly, and this box reports cudaDevAttrHostRegisterReadOnlySupported = 0,
so a read-only GGUF mapping can never be page-locked. That is true, and it is what kills the
GenerelSchwerz moe-cache fork here.

THE WAY AROUND IT: open the file READ-WRITE and map it PAGE_READWRITE. Plain (writable)
registration IS supported (attr = 1). Nothing ever writes through the mapping.
Measured (scratchpad/pinmap.cu), DMA straight out of a registered file mapping, no copy:
    4 GiB  registered in 0.7 s   11.67 GB/s
    8 GiB  registered in 1.2 s   10.89 GB/s
   12 GiB  registered in 1.7 s    9.60 GB/s
   14 GiB  registered in 1.9 s    8.32 GB/s      (14 GiB is the ceiling, as for cudaHostAlloc)
against a 12.8 GB/s link ceiling. So the slabs can be uploaded in place.

bench/mkhot.py writes the top N GiB of experts, ranked by the undecayed counts in a v2 heat
file, into one file plus an index. LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_HOT=<path> maps it read-write, pages it
in with the I/O threads, locks it, and the worker then uploads a hot slab straight from the
mapping: no read, no staging, one DRAM pass, and a locked page can never fault to disk.

TWO THINGS THAT MATTER AND ARE EASY TO GET WRONG
 1. The file must be DISJOINT from the VRAM cache. Built from rank 0 it is mostly a second
    copy of what L1 already holds: 11.65 -> 12.16 (+4%). Built from rank 67 per layer
    (= --moe-stream-cache slots) it is the tier behind L1: 11.82 -> 12.28/12.39.
    mkhot.py takes the skip count as its 4th argument.
 2. The index MUST be keyed on the slab's source location (split index + byte offset), never
    on (layer, weight position). llama.cpp registers a layer's streamed weights in model
    builder order, and qwen4exp creates ffn_down_exps BEFORE gate/up. The first version
    assumed gate/up/down, served the wrong tensor for every slab, measured 48.03 tok/s - the
    no-I/O ceiling, because the corrupted routing collapsed onto a handful of experts - and
    emitted "!!!!!!!!" for every prompt. bench/qual.py exists to catch exactly this: six
    factual checks plus a diff against a control run. ALWAYS RUN IT BEFORE BELIEVING A NUMBER.

Measured with the file in place (matched L1 states, cache 6, io 4, --poll 0):
      read summed    289 -> 109 ms/tok   (-62%; 72% of slab reads become hot hits)
      stall           67 -> 55 ms/tok
      8-prompt bench 11.8 -> 12.7 tok/s
      conversation   10.2/10.5 -> 10.6/10.8 median, FIRST turns 5.5/9.6 -> 12.6/14.1
Sizing: 11 GiB is the sweet spot. 14 GiB gives +1% but takes 157 s to start and leaves the OS
2.6 GiB of page cache. The file is locked RAM, so it is zero-sum with the page cache in
capacity terms. It wins because a hot hit costs one DRAM pass instead of three.

