=== THE TWO NUMBERS THAT BOUND EVERYTHING (check these before any new idea) ===
LLAMA_MOE_STREAM_NOWAIT=1 maps every requested expert onto slot (id % n_slots) and loads
nothing. The output is garbage by construction; the point is the clock:
      47.4 tok/s  =  21 ms per token of GPU compute + graph with the expert stall removed.
So of an ~80 ms token, 21 ms is compute and ~59 ms is expert I/O, and 47 tok/s is the ceiling
that no amount of I/O work could ever pass.
Second bound: 267 MiB of expert weight crosses PCIe every token at the measured 70% L1 hit
rate. Gen3 x16 tops out at 12.8 GB/s (scratchpad/h2d.cu), so 21 ms of that 59 is pure link
time. It cannot be removed without a higher L1 hit rate, which needs VRAM this card does not
have: cache 8 GiB falls off the driver's spill cliff to 3.85 tok/s.

=== RAM SENSITIVITY, MEASURED (bench/ramslope.py + bench/hog.py) ===
Holding N GiB of touched private memory to shrink the page cache:
      0 GiB   standby 15.3  disk  70.0 MiB/tok   10.79 tok/s
      2 GiB   standby 12.0  disk  92.2 MiB/tok    9.87
      4 GiB   standby  9.6  disk 107.9 MiB/tok    9.80
      6 GiB   standby  7.5  disk 129.7 MiB/tok    8.80
=> about 0.17 tok/s per GiB of page cache. This is the number that says chasing RAM is worth
far less than it looks: recovering the entire ~8 GiB the GPU driver's WDDM backing holds
would be worth about 1.4 tok/s. More RAM is NOT the big lever it is usually assumed to be
on this workload, because the marginal experts it buys are cold ones.

